<Base title="Affiliate disclosure | The Fault Code" description="How The Fault Code uses affiliate links, including Amazon Associates, and how they do not affect what the code pages say.">
<div class="wrap prose"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><ol><li><a href="/">Home</a></li><li aria-current="page">Affiliate disclosure</li></ol></nav><div class="card" style="margin-top:12px"><h1>Affiliate disclosure</h1>{affiliatesActive ? <p>Some links on this site are affiliate links. If you buy something or book a service through one of them, we may earn a commission at no extra cost to you. Pages with affiliate links say so near the top and in the footer, and the links are marked as sponsored.</p> : <p>At the moment, no link on this site earns us a commission. If we join affiliate programs in the future, links that earn a commission will be marked as sponsored and this page will say so.</p>}
{affiliates.amazon.enabled && <><h2>Amazon Associates</h2><p>{AMAZON_STATEMENT}</p><p>The "Shop on Amazon" buttons on code pages open Amazon search results for a generic part name, such as "furnace flame sensor" or "washing machine drain hose". They are not product recommendations. Check the part number against your furnace's manual or the old part before you buy, and leave gas valve and other gas-train work to a licensed technician.</p></>}
<p>Affiliate links do not change which codes we list or what we say about them. Code meanings come from the manufacturers' manuals.</p></div></div>
</Base>
